\details{
The update follows \code{online_cp()} of onlineBcp 0.1.4
(\url{https://github.com/ElsevierSoftwareX/SOFTX-D-21-00221}). At time \code{t}, for
every candidate location \code{j} from the last change point \code{tau} to \code{t},
\itemize{
\item \code{j < t}: \verb{w = Gamma(u + 1/2) / Gamma(u) * sqrt((t - j + 1) / (t - j + 2)) * (A + 2 beta)^u / (B + 2 beta)^(u + 1/2) / sqrt(pi)}, \verb{u = (t - j + 2 alpha) / 2},
with \code{A} and \code{B} the centred sums of squares of \code{x[(j + 1):t]} and
\code{x[(j + 1):(t + 1)]} (with denominators \code{t - j + 1} and \code{t - j + 2}), and
\verb{P(R = j) = w theta P_prev(R = j)};
\item \code{j = t}: \verb{w = 2^alpha Gamma(alpha + 1/2) / (sqrt(2 pi) (x[t + 1]^2 / 2 + beta)^(alpha + 1/2))},
and \verb{P(R = t) = w (1 - theta) sum(P_prev)}.
}

The code differs from Eq. 3 of the paper in the weight of a new segment,
\code{(x^2 / 2 + beta)} where the paper writes \verb{(x^2 / 2 + 2 beta)}; the code is
followed. With \code{numerics = "reference"}, \code{gamma()} overflows when
\verb{(t - j + 2 alpha + 1) / 2} exceeds about 171.6, and the weight, then
\code{NaN} or \code{Inf}, is set to 0 as in the code. The change points of the paper's
aCGH example at 341, 682 and 1023 come from this overflow; \code{"stable"}
avoids it.

The segments are built as \code{summary.BayesCP()} does. A change point is a
location whose posterior exceeds \code{threshold} at a step where the location
differs from that of the previous step. Segments of one observation are
merged with the previous segment of more than one, or with the next one at
the start. The interval of each segment mean is
\verb{mean +- qnorm(0.95) sd / sqrt(n)} (a 90\% interval; the paper calls it 95\%).
With \code{normality = TRUE}, the p-value of the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test of each
segment against the normal distribution with its mean and standard deviation
is added.

The cost of a step grows with the time since the last change point, so a
long sequence without changes takes quadratic time.
